.TH GPU 2
.SH NAME
gpuinit, gpufini, gpuresource, gputryresource, gpuopen, gpuemit, gpusubmit, gpuflush, gpuwait, gpuwrite, gpubind, gpushader, gpufloat \- VirGL file client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <gpu.h>
.PP
.B void gpuinit(Gpu *g, char *device)
.PP
.B void gpufini(Gpu *g)
.PP
.B u32int gpuresource(Gpu *g, char *description)
.PP
.B u32int gputryresource(Gpu *g, char *description)
.PP
.B int gpuopen(Gpu *g, u32int id, int mode)
.PP
.B u32int *gpuemit(Gpu *g, int op, int object, int words)
.PP
.B void gpusubmit(Gpu *g)
.PP
.B void gpuflush(Gpu *g)
.PP
.B void gpuwait(Gpu *g)
.PP
.B void gpuwrite(Gpu *g, u32int id, ulong offset, void *data, int bytes)
.PP
.B void gpubind(Gpu *g, int type, int id)
.PP
.B void gpushader(Gpu *g, int id, int type, char *text)
.PP
.B u32int gpufloat(float f)
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions encode the VirGL commands used by generated9's native
rendering clients. They expose the wire protocol rather than a graphics API.
.PP
.I Gpuinit
initializes caller-owned storage, opens
.IB device /clone
and the resulting context's command file.
Nil selects
.BR #∇ .
The device path is copied.
The resulting control descriptor and context slot are available as
.B g->ctl
and
.BR g->slot .
.I Gpufini
waits for submitted work, closes the context files and frees the copied path.
Commands still in the client buffer are discarded.
The caller must close resource descriptors separately.
Closing all context files releases its host resources.
.PP
.I Gpuresource
sends a
.B resource
control command with the supplied description and returns the allocated ID.
.I Gputryresource
does the same, but returns zero and sets the error string if the allocation
command fails, allowing the caller to release resources before retrying.
.I Gpuopen
opens that resource with the given mode; the caller owns the descriptor.
Resource uploads, downloads and removal use control commands documented in
.IR gpu (3).
.PP
.I Gpuemit
appends a command header and the requested number of zeroed payload words
to the context's 65536-word command buffer, returning the payload pointer.
If the command does not fit in the remaining space, it first submits the
buffer. A single command payload must contain fewer than 65536 words.
Fill the returned payload before appending another command.
.I Gpusubmit
writes the accumulated commands and waits for device completion.
The buffer is then reusable.
.I Gpuflush
also submits the buffer, but returns before GPU completion. The kernel owns
its copy of the commands; the client can immediately prepare the next batch.
There is one request in flight across all contexts, so the next submission
may wait for the previous one.
.I Gpuwait
waits for submitted work without submitting the client buffer.
.I Gpusubmit
waits even when that buffer is empty.
.PP
.I Gpuwrite
copies bytes into inline writes to a buffer resource at the given byte offset,
splitting them across command buffers as needed. The writes remain queued
until submission. They update the host resource; use a download control
command before reading the resource's guest backing.
VirGL inline writes are unsynchronized. Use disjoint ranges for pending draws
and call
.I gpusubmit
before overwriting a range those draws use.
Control commands are not queued with these encoded commands; submit pending
commands before a dependent download or resource removal.
.PP
.I Gpubind
appends an object binding.
.I Gpushader
appends creation and binding of a shader from NUL-terminated TGSI text,
with a limit of 2048 translated TGSI tokens.
Type zero selects a vertex shader and one selects a fragment shader.
The host compiles the shader; no guest shader compiler is used.
.I Gpufloat
returns a float's 32-bit wire representation.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libgpu
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gputest (1),
.IR tinygl (2),
.IR gpu (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are fatal except for allocation failure in
.IR gputryresource .
The client is not thread-safe.
.SH BUGS
The interface is experimental and exposes a subset of VirGL v1 encoding.
It assumes little-endian 32-bit words and IEEE floating-point values and
has been tested on amd64. It does not validate the semantics of command
payloads. Device permissions, bounds and host limitations are described in
.IR gpu (3).
